      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough’s name is a playful swap of the Berwind family name, turning “Berwind” into “Windber.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windber%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Windber stands alone among southwestern Pennsylvania’s company towns, built around bituminous mining in the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://explorepahistory.com/hmarker.php%3FmarkerId=1-A-2D2.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Vintage Electric Streetcar Company, locally dubbed the “trolley graveyard,” still marks Windber’s rail heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
